# Mobile UI/UX Text Guidelines

## General Principles
- Write in plain, user-centric language; avoid jargon or internal terms.
- Be concise: keep text to the minimum that conveys meaning.
- Address the user directly (“you”) or use imperative verbs; keep tone conversational and friendly.
- Frame statements positively and solution-oriented; never blame the user.
- Maintain consistent terminology, capitalization style, and voice across the app.

## Microcopy (Buttons, Labels, Tooltips)
- Use strong action verbs + object where needed (e.g., **Save changes**, **Upload file**).
- Avoid vague labels (“OK”, “Click here”); be specific and descriptive.
- Keep button text 1-3 words; shorten only if clarity is preserved.
- Follow platform conventions (e.g., **Sign in**, **Tap**); prefer sentence case.
- Ensure consistent terms for the same concept everywhere.

## System Messages (Errors, Notifications, Confirmations)
- State what happened and (if useful) how to fix or proceed in ≤ 2 short sentences.
- Place the message near the relevant UI element/action.
- Use neutral language; do not blame or scold the user.
- Offer an actionable next step or retry when possible.
- Reserve exclamation points for genuine success celebrations; never use them in errors.

## Onboarding & Empty States
- Emphasize benefits and first actions, not implementation details.
- One idea per screen: headline ≤ 7 words, body ≤ 2 short sentences.
- Provide a clear next step (button/instruction) in every empty state.
- Allow users to skip onboarding; note where help can be found later.

## Style & Formatting
- Prefer sentence case for headings and labels; remain consistent.
- Use numerals for all numbers (e.g., **3 files**, **24 h**).
- Contractions are encouraged; avoid ALL CAPS unless mandated by visual design.
- Omit periods in single-sentence labels, buttons, or toast messages.
- Use inclusive language; avoid gendered terms, idioms, or culture-specific references.

## Verification Checklist (for the Agent)
- [ ] Text is understandable on first read.
- [ ] No unnecessary words or repetition.
- [ ] Action labels contain clear, specific verbs.
- [ ] Error/help messages give the user a path forward.
- [ ] Tone matches context (neutral for errors, encouraging for successes).
